For `=`: clear effective_who bits, then set perm & effective_who\n\nLet me verify this formula: clear the effective_who bits from the current mode, then OR in the permission bits masked by effective_who. But when I work through the math with effective_who = 0o755, I get 0o666 instead of the observed 0o644. My model isn't matching the actual behavior, so I need to look at how GNU coreutils actually implements this—it seems the umask is being applied to the permission bits themselves rather than just determining which bits can be modified. the clear mask is still the full `who` (all classes). Let me verify with a couple examples: when adding execute permissions with umask 022 on a file with mode 0o644, the value gets masked by the inverse umask, giving 0o111, which ORs with the base to produce 0o755. Similarly, removing write permissions from 0o777 with the same umask masks the value to 0o200, which then gets cleared from the original mode to yield 0o577. So the pattern holds: the value is always masked by the inverse umask, while the clear operation uses the full `who` mask. Now I'm verifying the logic for the minus operator with explicit who masks—when removing write permissions from a specific user or group, the value gets masked by the who bits before being subtracted from the mode. I should also check how the equals operator behaves in these cases to make sure the model is consistent. Verifying my chmod mode calculation model against actual test results—the formula `(base & ~who) | (value & who)` is holding up across multiple cases like `a=rx`, `g=rx`, `o=`, and compound expressions like `u=rwx,go=rx`. The model checks out for those cases. Now I need to handle the special permission bits—setuid, setgid, and sticky—which operate outside the standard 9-bit permission model and require separate handling in the implementation. Writing the mode parsing functions... Writing the symbolic mode parser... Writing permission parsing logic... Finishing the mode operation logic...\n\nI'm realizing this is getting quite lengthy, so I should reconsider how to organize the code. The main entry point needs to handle parsing the mode string, converting octal representations, and processing symbolic notation along with its helper functions.\n\nFor the symbolic mode parser specifically, I need to verify it's actually required by the tests — the specification mentions both octal and symbolic modes are supported, and since the differential tests compare against the standard mkdir, I'll need to implement both.\n\nNow I'm hitting a snag with the flag parsing: when I encounter the `-m` flag in short form, I need to grab the mode string either from the remaining characters in that flag or from the next argument, but the current `parseShortFlags` function doesn't have access to subsequent arguments. I'll need to refactor the short flag handling to support this properly.\n\nRefactoring flag parsing... I'm realizing this approach is getting tangled. Let me refactor `parseShortFlags` to return both the stopping index and whether it encountered `-m`, so I can handle the mode argument more cleanly without all this nested logic.\n\nWriting refactored parser... Writing flag parsing logic... Writing the run function... Writing the chmod logic...\n\nI'm realizing the error handling for invalid modes needs to match gmkdir's format—the parseMode function should return just the mode string in the error message, and the main function will add the prefix. Let me verify how the error flows through the run function to make sure the formatting is consistent. I need to verify the error output format against gmkdir's actual behavior—the differential tests will catch any mismatches. For mode parsing errors, I'm using `fmt.Errorf(\"invalid mode '%s'\", modeStr)` which produces the right format without needing a \"Try --help\" line (that's only for option parsing). Now I'm thinking through how strconv.ParseUint handles both \"0755\" and \"755\" formats for octal mode strings.\n\nDefining the mode parsing function... I'm realizing the ParseUint function with base 8 and 32-bit width could theoretically accept values way larger than the 12-bit Unix mode limit of 07777, so I need that bounds check. I'll defer testing edge cases like leading zeros to the differential tests and proceed with capping at 07777, and for the default mode, I'll just use the standard os.Mkdir behavior without a special default.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full implementation: I need to modify the options struct to track whether a mode was explicitly set, update the run function to parse the mode once upfront, and adjust mkdirParents to apply chmod only to the final directory when a mode is specified. I'll also need helper functions to parse octal and symbolic modes, convert Unix mode values to FileMode, and handle umask operations.\n\nWriting implementation code... I need to verify that gmkdir supports both `--mode 0755` and `--mode=0755` syntax, and also check how it handles edge cases like `-m -p dir` where the next flag might be mistaken for the mode value.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":214,"cache_read_input_tokens":37829,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":214},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93224758-ad13-4d8f-a80f-51b3086b2052","uuid":"17882598-7a21-477a-a0fd-c8fb7872943e"}
